<section class="lesson-card" id="selection-procedure">
<h2>12. A practical selection procedure</h2>
<ol class="steps">
<li><strong>Define the observation.</strong> State whether one observation is a pipe, a 100 m length, an hour, a surface area, or the gap between events.</li>
<li><strong>Define the response.</strong> Is it a yes/no classification, a whole-number event count, or a continuous waiting time or distance?</li>
<li><strong>Check the data-generating mechanism.</strong> Fixed two-outcome trials point to Binomial; events in exposure point to Poisson; a gap to the next event points to Exponential.</li>
<li><strong>Align units.</strong> Convert λ and t to compatible exposure units before calculating μ = λt or e<sup>−λt</sup>.</li>
<li><strong>Check assumptions.</strong> Evaluate independence, constant p or λ, consistent exposure, clustering, aging, seasonality, and inspection consistency.</li>
<li><strong>Select the probability wording.</strong> Decide whether the question asks for exactly, at most, at least, between, by a time, or beyond a time.</li>
<li><strong>Calculate and validate.</strong> Show substitution and intermediate values; compare with software or a second method where practical.</li>
<li><strong>Interpret and document.</strong> State the result in operational language, identify limitations, and record the model, parameters, units, and assumptions.</li>
</ol>
</section>
<section class="lesson-card" id="interpretation">
<h2>13. When you see this, it means this</h2>
<div class="table-wrap"><table>
<thead><tr><th>When you see this</th><th>It means this</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td>A fixed n with pass/fail outcomes</td><td>The selected-outcome count is bounded by n; consider a Binomial model.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Multiple events can occur in one interval</td><td>The response is an event count rather than a defective-unit count; consider Poisson.</td></tr>
<tr><td>A continuous time or distance until the next event</td><td>The response is a gap; consider Exponential if the event rate is stable.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Large λ</td><td>Events are frequent; expected counts are higher and average gaps are shorter.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Small λ</td><td>Events are rare; expected counts are lower and average gaps are longer.</td></tr>
<tr><td>μ = λt</td><td>Expected count grows in direct proportion to the observation opportunity.</td></tr>
<tr><td>1/λ</td><td>The long-run average waiting time or distance between consecutive events.</td></tr>
<tr><td>P(T &gt; t)</td><td>The probability of seeing zero events during the next t exposure units.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Poisson variance far above the mean</td><td>Clustering, mixed rates, or omitted exposure effects may be present.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Opportunity is equal across samples</td><td>A c chart can compare raw defect counts.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Opportunity varies across samples</td><td>A u chart should compare defects per unit with sample-specific limits.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Event rate changes over time</td><td>A single stationary Poisson/Exponential model may be inappropriate or require stratification.</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table></div>
</section>
<section class="lesson-card" id="common-mistakes">
<h2>14. Common mistakes and exam traps</h2>
<div class="mistake-group">
<h3>Calculation mistakes</h3>
<div class="grid-2">
<div class="trap-card"><span class="tag calc">Calculation</span><strong>Misaligning rate and exposure units</strong><p><strong>Why it is wrong:</strong> λt is only meaningful when the units cancel correctly.</p><p><strong>Recognize it:</strong> λ is entered per 100 m while t is entered in metres.</p><p><strong>Avoid it:</strong> convert λ to events per metre or express t in 100 m units before multiplying.</p></div>
<div class="trap-card"><span class="tag calc">Calculation</span><strong>Using the wrong tail</strong><p><strong>Why it is wrong:</strong> P(X ≥ x) is not the same as P(X ≤ x).</p><p><strong>Recognize it:</strong> the wording says “at least” or “more than,” but the cumulative lower tail is reported directly.</p><p><strong>Avoid it:</strong> translate the wording before calculating and use complements carefully.</p></div>
</div>
</div>
<div class="mistake-group">
<h3>Interpretation mistakes</h3>
<div class="grid-2">
<div class="trap-card"><span class="tag interp">Interpretation</span><strong>Assuming every count is Poisson</strong><p><strong>Why it is wrong:</strong> failed units out of fixed n are Binomial, while defects in exposure are Poisson.</p><p><strong>Recognize it:</strong> each unit contributes at most one selected outcome.</p><p><strong>Avoid it:</strong> ask how the count was generated, not merely whether it is a whole number.</p></div>
<div class="trap-card"><span class="tag interp">Interpretation</span><strong>Treating a control-limit signal as a root cause</strong><p><strong>Why it is wrong:</strong> a signal indicates unusual variation; it does not identify why it occurred.</p><p><strong>Recognize it:</strong> the conclusion jumps directly from “outside limits” to a specific cause.</p><p><strong>Avoid it:</strong> investigate process history, measurement, materials, methods, and operating conditions.</p></div>
</div>
</div>
<div class="mistake-group">
<h3>Assumption mistakes</h3>
<div class="grid-2">
<div class="trap-card"><span class="tag assume">Assumption</span><strong>Ignoring clustering or changing rates</strong><p><strong>Why it is wrong:</strong> basic Poisson variance may be too small for a bursty or mixed-rate process.</p><p><strong>Recognize it:</strong> count variance greatly exceeds the mean or events occur in bursts.</p><p><strong>Avoid it:</strong> stratify the process and consider an overdispersed model.</p></div>
<div class="trap-card"><span class="tag assume">Assumption</span><strong>Assuming memoryless means equipment never ages</strong><p><strong>Why it is wrong:</strong> memorylessness follows from a constant hazard; real wear-out can increase hazard with age.</p><p><strong>Recognize it:</strong> older components fail more frequently.</p><p><strong>Avoid it:</strong> examine hazard behavior and consider Weibull or another reliability model.</p></div>
</div>
</div>
<div class="mistake-group">
<h3>Software-output mistakes</h3>
<div class="grid-2">
<div class="trap-card"><span class="tag software">Software</span><strong>Confusing density with probability</strong><p><strong>Why it is wrong:</strong> for a continuous Exponential variable, the density at one exact time is not the probability of that exact time.</p><p><strong>Recognize it:</strong> a software “probability density” value is interpreted as an interval probability.</p><p><strong>Avoid it:</strong> use cumulative or survival probabilities for practical time intervals and thresholds.</p></div>
<div class="trap-card"><span class="tag software">Software</span><strong>Entering Exponential rate where software expects scale</strong><p><strong>Why it is wrong:</strong> rate λ and scale 1/λ are reciprocals.</p><p><strong>Recognize it:</strong> Minitab asks for scale but the event rate is entered unchanged.</p><p><strong>Avoid it:</strong> calculate scale = 1/λ and verify the software parameterization.</p></div>
</div>
</div>
<div class="mistake-group">
<h3>Exam traps</h3>
<div class="grid-2">
<div class="trap-card"><span class="tag exam">Exam trap</span><strong>Defective versus defect</strong><p><strong>Why it is wrong:</strong> a defective unit is a yes/no classification; a unit may contain multiple defects.</p><p><strong>Recognize it:</strong> the question alternates between “units failing” and “nonconformities found.”</p><p><strong>Avoid it:</strong> map defective to Binomial charts and defects to Poisson charts.</p></div>
<div class="trap-card"><span class="tag exam">Exam trap</span><strong>c chart versus u chart</strong><p><strong>Why it is wrong:</strong> raw counts are not comparable when inspection opportunity varies.</p><p><strong>Recognize it:</strong> sample length, area, or number of units changes.</p><p><strong>Avoid it:</strong> equal opportunity → c; unequal opportunity → u.</p></div>
</div>
</div>
</section>
<!-- EDIT: Assumption checker -->
<section class="lesson-card" id="assumptions">
<h2>15. Assumption checker</h2>
<p class="section-intro">A formula can be mathematically correct and still be operationally misleading. Use this checklist as a screening tool before relying on a model.</p>
<div class="interactive-shell">
<div class="control"><label for="assumptionModel">Model to assess</label><select id="assumptionModel"><option value="binomial">Binomial</option><option value="poisson">Poisson</option><option value="exponential">Exponential</option></select></div>
<div class="assumption-grid" id="assumptionList"></div>
<div class="button-row"><button class="btn" id="assumptionAllBtn" type="button">Mark all supported</button><button class="btn secondary" id="assumptionResetBtn" type="button">Clear checklist</button></div>
<p aria-live="polite" class="status-line warn" id="assumptionResult"></p>
</div>
<div class="table-wrap"><table><thead><tr><th>Diagnostic clue</th><th>What it may indicate</th><th>Next action</th></tr></thead><tbody>
<tr><td>Poisson count variance far above the mean</td><td>Clustering, mixed rates, or omitted exposure effects</td><td>Stratify the data; consider negative-binomial or another overdispersed count model.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Poisson count variance far below the mean</td><td>Regular spacing, process constraints, or dependence</td><td>Review the event definition and whether occurrences inhibit one another.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Failure rate increases with age</td><td>Constant-hazard Exponential assumption is violated</td><td>Consider Weibull or a reliability model that allows changing hazard.</td></tr>
<tr><td>Binomial p changes by supplier, shift, or product</td><td>Trials do not share one constant probability</td><td>Stratify or model the probability using relevant predictors.</td></tr>
</tbody></table></div>
</section>
<!-- EDIT: Software guidance -->
<section class="lesson-card" id="software">
<h2>16. Excel and Minitab guidance</h2>
<p>Software should execute the probability calculation; it should not choose the model for you. Define the random variable, align units, and confirm assumptions first.</p>
<div class="sheet-grid">
<div class="software-card"><h3>Excel</h3>
<p><strong>Binomial exact:</strong></p><code class="code-line">=BINOM.DIST(x, n, p, FALSE)</code><p class="small">Returns P(X = x).</p>
<p><strong>Binomial cumulative:</strong></p><code class="code-line">=BINOM.DIST(x, n, p, TRUE)</code><p class="small">Returns P(X ≤ x). For P(X ≥ x), use <code>1-BINOM.DIST(x-1,n,p,TRUE)</code>.</p>
<p><strong>Poisson exact:</strong></p><code class="code-line">=POISSON.DIST(x, mean, FALSE)</code><p class="small">Returns P(X = x), where mean = μ = λt.</p>
<p><strong>Poisson cumulative:</strong></p><code class="code-line">=POISSON.DIST(x, mean, TRUE)</code><p class="small">Returns P(X ≤ x). For P(X ≥ x), use <code>1-POISSON.DIST(x-1,mean,TRUE)</code>.</p>
<p><strong>Exponential cumulative:</strong></p><code class="code-line">=EXPON.DIST(t, lambda, TRUE)</code><p class="small">Returns P(T ≤ t). For P(T &gt; t), use <code>1-EXPON.DIST(t,lambda,TRUE)</code> or <code>EXP(-lambda*t)</code>.</p>
<div class="callout warn"><strong>Common Excel mistake:</strong> entering a rate per 100 m while t is measured in metres. Convert the units before multiplying λt.</div>
</div>
<div class="software-card"><h3>Minitab</h3>
<p><strong>Probability calculations:</strong> choose <em>Calc → Probability Distributions</em>, then select Binomial, Poisson, or Exponential.</p>
<ol>
<li>Select <strong>Probability</strong> for an exact discrete probability or a continuous density value.</li>
<li>Select <strong>Cumulative probability</strong> for P(X ≤ x) or P(T ≤ t).</li>
<li>Enter the correct parameter: n and p for Binomial; mean μ for Poisson; scale 1/λ for Exponential.</li>
<li>For an upper tail, subtract the appropriate cumulative probability from 1 and check whether the boundary is inclusive.</li>
<li>Store the result in a constant or column when documenting repeated calculations.</li>
</ol>
<p><strong>Output fields that matter:</strong> the entered parameters, input value, probability type, and returned probability. Record all four so another analyst can reproduce the result.</p>
<p><strong>Attribute charts:</strong> choose <em>Stat → Control Charts → Attribute Charts</em>. Use <strong>C</strong> for constant opportunity and <strong>U</strong> when opportunity varies; supply subgroup sizes or opportunity values for the u chart.</p>
<div class="callout warn"><strong>Common Minitab mistake:</strong> confusing Exponential scale with rate. The scale is the mean waiting time, 1/λ.</div>
</div>
</div>
</section>
<section class="lesson-card" id="practice">
<h2>17. Practice activity</h2>
<p><strong>Try it first:</strong> answer each question before revealing the worked solution.</p>
<p>A coating line inspects 30 panels. Each panel has a 5% chance of being rejected, while pinholes occur at an average rate of 4 per 200 m².</p>
<ol><li>Which distribution models the number of rejected panels?</li><li>What is the expected number of rejected panels?</li><li>Which distribution models the number of pinholes in the next 200 m²?</li><li>Which distribution models the inspected area until the next pinhole?</li><li>What is the mean area between pinholes?</li></ol>
<button aria-controls="practiceAnswer" aria-expanded="false" class="btn" id="revealPracticeBtn" type="button">Reveal answer</button>
<div class="reveal" hidden id="practiceAnswer"><p><strong>1. Binomial.</strong> There are 30 fixed yes-or-no panel trials.</p><p><strong>2. Expected rejected panels = 1.5.</strong> E(X) = np = 30 × 0.05 = 1.5.</p><p><strong>3. Poisson.</strong> The response is the number of pinhole events in a fixed area.</p><p><strong>4. Exponential.</strong> The response is the continuous area until the next pinhole.</p><p><strong>5. Mean gap = 50 m².</strong> Rate = 4/200 = 0.02 per m², so E(T) = 1/λ = 1/0.02 = 50 m².</p></div>
<div class="extension-practice">
<h3>Extension: choose a chart and test a waiting-time claim</h3>
<p>Eight inspected coil lengths are 100, 150, 80, 200, 120, 100, 250, and 100 m. Their defect counts are 2, 5, 1, 7, 3, 2, 9, and 2. The long-run rate is estimated at 0.03 defect per metre.</p>
<ol><li>Should the team use a c chart or u chart?</li><li>What is the probability of travelling more than 50 m before the next defect under the Exponential model?</li><li>State one assumption that must be checked.</li></ol>
<button aria-controls="extensionAnswer" aria-expanded="false" class="btn secondary" id="revealExtensionBtn" type="button">Reveal extension answer</button>
<div class="reveal" hidden id="extensionAnswer"><p><strong>1. Use a u chart.</strong> Inspected lengths vary, so raw counts have unequal opportunities. Plot defects per metre or per 100 m with limits that reflect each length.</p><p><strong>2. P(T &gt; 50) = e<sup>−0.03×50</sup> = e<sup>−1.5</sup> ≈ 0.2231.</strong> There is about a 22.31% chance of more than 50 m without the next defect.</p><p><strong>3. Check that the defect rate is reasonably constant and events are not clustered.</strong> A bursty process can invalidate the basic Poisson–Exponential assumptions.</p></div>
</div>
</section>
<section class="lesson-card" id="quiz">
<h2>18. Mini quiz</h2>
<form id="quizForm">
<div class="quiz-question" data-answer="b"><p><strong>1. Which statement correctly distinguishes Binomial and Poisson counts?</strong></p><label class="option"><input name="q1" type="radio" value="a"/> Binomial counts are continuous, while Poisson counts are discrete.</label><label class="option"><input name="q1" type="radio" value="b"/> Binomial counts selected outcomes in fixed two-outcome trials; Poisson counts events in an exposure interval.</label><label class="option"><input name="q1" type="radio" value="c"/> Poisson counts can never exceed the sample size.</label><div aria-live="polite" class="feedback" role="status">Both models produce discrete counts, but the data-generating mechanisms are different.</div></div>
<div class="quiz-question" data-answer="c"><p><strong>2. Defects occur at 2 per 100 m. What is the expected count in 300 m?</strong></p><label class="option"><input name="q2" type="radio" value="a"/> 0.67 defects</label><label class="option"><input name="q2" type="radio" value="b"/> 2 defects</label><label class="option"><input name="q2" type="radio" value="c"/> 6 defects</label><div aria-live="polite" class="feedback" role="status">Use μ = λt. Three 100 m exposure units give μ = 2 × 3 = 6 expected defects.</div></div>
<div class="quiz-question" data-answer="a"><p><strong>3. What does P(T &gt; 75) = 0.223 mean in the defect-distance example?</strong></p><label class="option"><input name="q3" type="radio" value="a"/> There is a 22.3% chance of travelling more than 75 m before the next defect.</label><label class="option"><input name="q3" type="radio" value="b"/> Exactly 22.3 defects are expected in 75 m.</label><label class="option"><input name="q3" type="radio" value="c"/> Every 75 m section contains a defect.</label><div aria-live="polite" class="feedback" role="status">The Exponential survival probability is the chance of zero events during the next 75 m, which is equivalent to waiting more than 75 m for the next event.</div></div>
<div class="quiz-question" data-answer="c"><p><strong>4. Which observation most strongly challenges a basic Poisson model?</strong></p><label class="option"><input name="q4" type="radio" value="a"/> Counts are non-negative whole numbers.</label><label class="option"><input name="q4" type="radio" value="b"/> The inspection opportunity is clearly defined.</label><label class="option"><input name="q4" type="radio" value="c"/> Events occur in bursts and the count variance greatly exceeds the mean.</label><div aria-live="polite" class="feedback" role="status">Bursting and strong overdispersion indicate clustering or mixed rates, which violate the simplest independent, constant-rate Poisson process.</div></div>
<div class="quiz-question" data-answer="b"><p><strong>5. Eight coils have different inspected lengths. Which chart should compare their defect results?</strong></p><label class="option"><input name="q5" type="radio" value="a"/> c chart using raw counts</label><label class="option"><input name="q5" type="radio" value="b"/> u chart using defects per inspection opportunity</label><label class="option"><input name="q5" type="radio" value="c"/> np chart using the number defective</label><div aria-live="polite" class="feedback" role="status">Use a u chart when opportunity varies because it plots defects per unit and uses limits that reflect each sample’s exposure.</div></div>
<div class="button-row"><button class="btn" type="submit">Submit quiz</button><button class="btn secondary" id="resetQuizBtn" type="button">Reset quiz</button></div>
<div aria-live="polite" id="quizScore"></div>
</form>
</section>
<section class="lesson-card" id="summary">
<h2>19. Final summary</h2>
<ul>
<li>Binomial models selected outcomes in a fixed number of yes-or-no trials.</li>
<li>Poisson models random event counts in a defined interval or opportunity.</li>
<li>Exponential models the continuous waiting time or distance until the next Poisson event.</li>
<li>Binomial counts are bounded by n; Poisson counts are not bounded by a fixed trial count.</li>
<li>For Binomial data, E(X) = np and Var(X) = np(1-p).</li>
<li>For Poisson data, μ = λt and both the mean and variance equal μ under the ideal model.</li>
<li>For Exponential data, the mean gap is 1/λ and P(T &gt; t) = e<sup>−λt</sup>.</li>
<li>p and np charts are Binomial; c and u charts are Poisson.</li>
</ul>
<div class="big-memory">Decision rule: fixed yes-or-no trials → Binomial; event count in an interval → Poisson; waiting time or distance → Exponential.</div>
<p><strong>Remember this:</strong> <em>Count selected trials, count random events, or measure the spaces.</em></p>
<p><strong>Suggested next lesson:</strong> Attribute control charts—choosing among p, np, c, and u charts.</p>
</section>
